emains valid for operator-norm
+asymptotic representations into $\U(A_n)$, with normalized
+Hilbert--Schmidt norm replaced by the trace $2$-norm.  Consequently
+the finite-normal and central-involution obstructions remain valid for
+homomorphisms into
+\[
+ \U\!\left(\prod_nA_n\big/\bigoplus_nA_n\right).
+\]
+This applies in particular when the $A_n$ are commutative or AF
+(and hence when they are UHF).
+
+Write $D_n=\mathcal D_{\tau_n}(A_n)$.  For a coordinate unitary $v$,
+$\operatorname{Ad}v=L_vR_{v^*}\in D_n$, and operator-norm
+multiplicative defects give operator-norm defects for these adjoint
+actions.  The norm ultraproduct $\prod_\omega D_n$ is finite.  Indeed,
+if an element is an isometry in the ultraproduct, a lift $(x_n)$
+satisfies $x_n^*x_n\to1$; polar correction produces coordinate
+isometries $v_n$ with $\|v_n-x_n\|\to0$, and finiteness of $D_n$
+makes each $v_n$ unitary.  Thus the lifted element is unitary in the
+ultraproduct.  Its natural representation on the Hilbert-space
+ultraproduct is faithful, since the norm of a represented class is the
+ultralimit of the coordinate operator norms.
+
+The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:kazhdan-transport} can now be run inside
+$\prod_\omega D_n$: its Kazhdan spectral projection belongs to this
+finite algebra, so $P\le VPV^*$ and $P\sim VPV^*$ again imply
+$P=VPV^*$.  The corner argument used for the central-involution
+obstruction is stable under the same hypothesis.  If $0\ne q\in A$ is
+a projection and the trace on $qAq$ is renormalized, then under the
+natural identification of $L^2(qAq)$ with
+$(L_qR_q)L^2(A,\tau)$ its left--right algebra is a unital subalgebra of
+\[
+ (L_qR_q)\,\mathcal D_\tau(A)\,(L_qR_q),
+\]
+which is finite.
+
+For commutative $A$, left and right multiplication agree, so
+$\mathcal D_\tau(A)$ is commutative.  If $A$ is AF, the commuting left
+and right representations induce a surjection
+\[
+ A\otimes_{\max}A^{\mathrm{op}}\longrightarrow\mathcal D_\tau(A).
+\]
+Since AF algebras are nuclear,
+$A\otimes_{\max}A^{\mathrm{op}}=A\otimes_{\min}A^{\mathrm{op}}$ is AF;
+therefore its image is AF and in particular finite.
+
+
+\paragraph{A dimension--defect criterion for hyperlinearity.}
+Let $G$ be countable and let $F_1\subseteq F_2\subseteq\cdots$ be
+finite sets with $1\in F_n$ and $\bigcup_nF_n=G$.  Suppose there are
+maps $V_n\colon G\to\U(d_n)$ and numbers $\varepsilon_n>0$ such that
+$V_n(1)=1$,
+\[
+ \|V_n(gh)-V_n(g)V_n(h)\|\le\varepsilon_n
+ \quad(g,h,gh\in F_n),
+\]
+\[
+ \|V_n(g)-V_n(h)\|\ge1
+ \quad(g,h\in F_n,\ g\ne h),
+ \qquad
+ d_n\varepsilon_n\longrightarrow0.
+\]
+Then $G$ is hyperlinear.
+
+Put $\widetilde V_n(g)=V_n(g)\oplus I_{d_n}$.  This changes neither the
+operator-norm multiplicative defect nor the separation.  For distinct
+$g,h\in F_n$, let $z=V_n(g)^*V_n(h)$ and
+$m_0=\tr_{d_n}(z)$.  Since $\|1-z\|\ge1$, some eigenvalue of $z$ is at
+distance at least $1$ from $1$, and hence
+\[
+ 2-2\operatorname{Re}m_0
+ =\frac1{d_n}\sum_j|\lambda_j-1|^2
+ \ge\frac1{d_n}.
+\]
+The normalized trace of $z\oplus I_{d_n}$ is $m=(1+m_0)/2$, so
+\[
+ 1-|m|^2\ge\frac1{4d_n},
+ \qquad
+ |m|\le \exp\!\left(-\frac1{8d_n}\right).
+\]
+With $p_n=8d_n$, the tensor powers satisfy
+\[
+ \left\|\widetilde V_n(g)^{\otimes p_n}
+       -\widetilde V_n(h)^{\otimes p_n}\right\|_2^2
+ =2-2\operatorname{Re}(m^{p_n})
+ \ge2(1-e^{-1}).
+\]
+A telescoping estimate bounds each multiplicative defect of the tensor
+powers by $p_n\varepsilon_n=8d_n\varepsilon_n\to0$.  These maps are
+therefore hyperlinear local models on the exhaustion $(F_n)$.
+
+
+\subsection*{Remaining questions}
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item Does MF imply hyperlinearity?  The dimension--defect criterion above
+applies when exhausting MF local models have the displayed uniform
+operator-norm separation and satisfy $d_n\varepsilon_n\to0$, but the
+CDE definition supplies no such dimension--error rate.  Can one obtain
+such models, or bypass the rate by another amplification or corner
+argument?
+\item How far does one-sided Kazhdan transport extend beyond matrix
+blocks?  The left--right finiteness argument above gives a
+sufficient left--right finiteness condition, while the constant
+coordinates $A_n=\Cred(E)$ above show that stable finiteness and
+faithful traces alone are insufficient.  Is there an intrinsic
+characterization of the faithfully tracial $C^*$-algebras satisfying
+this condition, or a larger natural class for which the obstruction
+still survives?
+\item For which groups or Kazhdan conjugation data do
+Theorems~\ref{thm:criterion},~\ref{thm:normal-kazhdan} and
+\ref{thm:projection-collapse} compute the MF residual exactly?  The
+group $E$ and its sign-free quotient give positive examples by the
+exact computation above.  What structural hypotheses force exactness in
+general?
+\end{enumerate}
 
